// ============================================================================
//  Imo LIVE — Firestore security rules  **  DRAFT FOR REVIEW  **
//  NOT DEPLOYED. Nothing in this repository reads this file.
// ============================================================================
//
//  This replaces the rules currently serving in the Firebase console:
//
//      match /{document=**} { allow read, write: if request.auth != null; }
//
//  which let any signed-in user read and write every document in the database
//  (all chats, all call records, all profiles, all status updates).
//
//  Read the "KNOWN GAPS" section at the bottom before deploying. Five rules
//  below are deliberately permissive because the *client* does something the
//  rules cannot verify; each one is marked `LOOPHOLE` with the app change that
//  would close it. Deploying this file as-is is a large improvement over the
//  current state, not a finished job.
//
//  Collected from the actual call sites (2026-09-29), not from documentation:
//    lib/core/services/firestore_chat_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/call_signaling_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/channel_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/points_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/remote_contact_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/firebase_auth_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/e2ee_service.dart
//    lib/core/services/push_notification_service.dart
//
//  RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-30 against the current tree, after the delete-for-
//  everyone, l10n and ad changes. Four corrections were found; each is marked
//  `CORRECTED 2026-09-30` inline. Two of them would have broken a feature:
//
//    A. `channelIsPrivate()` tested `secretKeyHash != ''` as a proxy for
//       privacy. An approval-gated private channel has `isPublic: false` and an
//       empty `secretKeyHash`, so its posts were treated as PUBLIC. Now tests
//       `isPublic` directly.                              -> see the helper
//
//    B. The `messages` update rule was sender-only. But `markChatRead()` and
//       `updateMessageStatus()` are driven by the RECIPIENT and rewrite a
//       message the recipient did not send. Every read receipt would have been
//       DENIED — and because `markChatRead()` catches the failure and falls back
//       to clearing only the local counter, it would have broken quietly.
//                                                          -> see messages
//
//    C. `reactions` read was open to any signed-in user. The only query is a
//       collection-group `where('uid', == uid)`, so own-only is both provable
//       and correct.                                        -> see reactions
//
//    D. `chats` delete was allowed for any participant. No call site deletes a
//       chat document (`firestore_chat_service.dart` has exactly one `.delete()`
//       and it is `deleteContact`). Now denied.             -> see chats
//
//  To deploy once reviewed:
//    1. Add a "firestore" section to firebase.json:
//         "firestore": { "rules": "firestore.rules", "indexes": "firestore.indexes.json" }
//    2. Rename this file to firestore.rules
//    3. firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
//
// ============================================================================

//  ==========================================================================
//  QUERY <-> RULE PROVABILITY  (the check that decides outages)
//  ==========================================================================
//
//  A rule that reads another document, or that depends on document data, cannot
//  be satisfied by a query that does not filter on the same condition. When that
//  happens Firestore rejects the ENTIRE query — every row, not just the
//  disallowed ones — and the screen shows "Missing or insufficient permissions".
//  A rule that is too strict is not "safe", it is an outage.
//
//  So every query in lib/ was enumerated and each was checked to IMPLY its rule.
//  There are thirteen. The ones marked ⚠️ are the ones to re-check if anyone
//  edits the code near them.
//
//   #  query                                            rule it must imply
//  ---  ---------------------------------------------   ---------------------
//   1   chats .where(participants arrayContains uid)     read: uid in participants
//       firestore_chat_service.dart:354                    -> EXACT, provable
//   2   chats/{id}/messages .orderBy(timestamp)          read: participant (parent get)
//       firestore_chat_service.dart:309                    -> subcollection: chatId is
//                                                             fixed per query, so the
//                                                             condition is constant
//   3   chats/{id}/messages .where(senderId !=)          read: participant
//          .where(status != read)                         -> as #2
//       firestore_chat_service.dart:512
//   4   calls .where(receiverId == uid)                  read: callerId == uid
//          .where(status == ringing)                             || receiverId == uid
//       call_signaling_service.dart:453                    -> the receiverId filter
//                                                             IMPLIES the OR. Do not
//                                                             narrow this to the
//                                                             receiver alone or the
//                                                             caller stops watching
//                                                             their own call.
//   5   status .where(userId whereIn [contacts])         read: signedIn()
//       firestore_chat_service.dart:671                    -> the rule is weaker than
//                                                             the query: provable. This
//                                                             is WHY status read cannot
//                                                             be a contacts check.
//   6   users/{uid}/notifications .where(read == false)  read: owner
//       points_service.dart:476                            -> subcollection, path-scoped
//   7   users/{uid}/points_transactions                  read: owner
//          .orderBy(createdAt)                            -> as #6
//       points_service.dart:129
//   8   collectionGroup(reactions) .where(uid == uid)    read: resource.data.uid == uid
//       channel_service.dart:717                           -> EXACT, provable. Widening
//                                                             to signedIn() would also
//                                                             work but leaks the
//                                                             reaction graph.
//   9   channels .where(monetizationStatus == pending)   read: signedIn()
//       channel_service.dart:446                           -> ⚠️ THE REASON CHANNEL READ
//                                                             MUST STAY OPEN. An
//                                                             unfiltered collection
//                                                             query cannot prove a
//                                                             membership rule; gating
//                                                             this path on
//                                                             isChannelMember would
//                                                             break the admin queue
//                                                             for everyone.
//  10   channels/{id}/posts .orderBy(createdAt)          read: owner || member
//       channel_service.dart:548                               || !isPrivate
//                                                          -> subcollection; every term
//                                                             is constant per query, so
//                                                             provable both for members
//                                                             and for non-members of a
//                                                             public channel
//  11   channels/{id}/join_requests .orderBy(createdAt)  read: requesterUid == uid
//       channel_service.dart:802                               || isChannelOwner
//                                                          -> ⚠️ ONLY PROVABLE FOR THE
//                                                             OWNER. For a non-owner the
//                                                             rule reduces to a
//                                                             per-document id check the
//                                                             query does not filter on,
//                                                             and Firestore rejects it.
//                                                             See the note below.
//  12   channels/{id}/analytics .orderBy(documentId)     read: isChannelOwner
//          .startAt().endAt()                             -> subcollection; the get() is
//       channel_service.dart:961                              constant per query
//  13   users .where(field >= prefix)                    read: signedIn()
//          .where(field < prefix + \uf8ff)                -> rule is weaker: provable.
//       remote_contact_service.dart:283                       This is why users read
//                                                             cannot be owner-only —
//                                                             it would break search.
//
//  ⚠️ #11 IS HELD UP BY AN APP-SIDE GUARD, NOT BY THESE RULES.
//  `channelJoinRequestsProvider` is watched from `channel_screen.dart:1136`,
//  which ANY viewer can open — but `_OwnerTools.build()` returns early with
//  `if (!isOwner) return const SizedBox.shrink();` BEFORE the `ref.watch`, so a
//  non-owner never runs the query. That guard is the only thing keeping #11
//  provable.
//
//  If anyone moves that `ref.watch` above the `isOwner` guard, non-owners start
//  running an unprovable query and the join-request count dies with a permission
//  error that looks nothing like the change that caused it. See GAP 8.
//  ==========================================================================

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers

    function signedIn() {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid != null;
    }

    function uid() {
      return request.auth.uid;
    }

    // True when the caller is one of the two parties on a call document.
    function isCallParty(data) {
      return signedIn() && (data.callerId == uid() || data.receiverId == uid());
    }

    // --- channels -----------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // NOTE: every one of these uses get()/exists() on a *different* path, so
    // they are only usable on subcollection rules. A collection-level query on
    // `channels` (e.g. the monetization queue) cannot be proven against them
    // and would be rejected outright — see the `channels` read rule below.

    function channelDoc(channelId) {
      return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/channels/$(channelId)).data;
    }

    function isChannelOwner(channelId) {
      return signedIn() && channelDoc(channelId).ownerId == uid();
    }

    function isChannelMember(channelId) {
      return signedIn()
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/channels/$(channelId)/followers/$(uid()));
    }

    // A channel is private when `isPublic` is false.
    //
    // CORRECTED 2026-09-30. This used to test `secretKeyHash != ''`, which is
    // only a PROXY for privacy. `Channel` (lib/core/models/group.dart) carries
    // BOTH `isPublic` and `secretKeyHash`, and `isPublic` is the field the UI
    // actually branches on (`bool get isPrivate => !isPublic`). An
    // approval-gated private channel has `isPublic: false` and an EMPTY
    // `secretKeyHash` — so the proxy called it public and its posts were
    // readable by any signed-in user. Both fields are in `Channel.toJson`, so
    // the direct test is available and exact.
    function channelIsPrivate(channelId) {
      return channelDoc(channelId).get('isPublic', true) == false;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ users/{uid}
    //
    // The profile document doubles as the public directory: findByUid(),
    // watchProfile() and the prefix `search()` all read *other* users' docs by
    // design, so read has to be open to any signed-in user.
    //
    // LOOPHOLE (read): Firestore rules cannot hide individual fields, so this
    // also exposes `fcmToken`, `publicKey` and `points` to every signed-in
    // user. Closing it needs a schema change, not a rule change — see GAP 1.
    match /users/{userId} {

      allow read: if signedIn();

      // Ordinary self-write: profile edits, FCM token, published public key.
      allow create: if signedIn() && userId == uid();

      // LOOPHOLE (write): a gift has to move `points` on *both* documents, and
      // sendGift() does it from the sender's client. Without this exception
      // gifting breaks; with it, any signed-in user can set any other user's
      // balance. The whole points economy is client-authoritative today, so
      // this exception makes an existing hole explicit rather than creating a
      // new one. See GAP 2.
      allow update: if signedIn()
        && (
          userId == uid()
          || request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
               .hasOnly(['points', 'updatedAt'])
        );

      allow delete: if signedIn() && userId == uid();

      // --- per-user private data: owner only, in both directions ------------

      // A user's own points history. The gift path writes one document into
      // the *recipient's* subcollection, so the create rule has to accept a
      // document whose counterparty is the caller.
      match /points_transactions/{txId} {
        allow read: if signedIn() && userId == uid();
        allow create: if signedIn()
          && (
            userId == uid()
            || request.resource.data.counterpartyId == uid()
          );
        allow update, delete: if signedIn() && userId == uid();
      }

      // In-app notifications. Same gift-write shape: the sender creates the
      // recipient's notification, and the payload records `fromUid`.
      match /notifications/{notificationId} {
        allow read: if signedIn() && userId == uid();
        allow create: if signedIn()
          && (
            userId == uid()
            || request.resource.data.fromUid == uid()
          );
        allow update, delete: if signedIn() && userId == uid();
      }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------ directory indexes
    //
    // `usernames/{handle}`, `emails/{email}` and `phones/{phone}` all hold
    // `{ uid: <the owner> }` and exist so the app can answer "does this handle
    // / email / phone have an account?" without scanning `users`.
    //
    // LOOPHOLE (read): open to any signed-in user, which is what the contact
    // discovery feature requires — and also what makes it an enumeration
    // oracle. Someone can test arbitrary emails and phone numbers against it.
    // See GAP 3.
    match /usernames/{handle} {
      allow read: if signedIn();
      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.uid == uid();
      allow update, delete: if signedIn() && resource.data.uid == uid();
    }

    match /emails/{email} {
      allow read: if signedIn();
      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.uid == uid();
      allow update, delete: if signedIn() && resource.data.uid == uid();
    }

    match /phones/{phone} {
      allow read: if signedIn();
      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.uid == uid();
      allow update, delete: if signedIn() && resource.data.uid == uid();
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ chats/{id}
    //
    // Membership is the `participants` array, which is also what watchChats()
    // filters on (`arrayContains: uid`) — so this rule is provable for that
    // query and the chat list keeps working.
    match /chats/{chatId} {

      allow read: if signedIn() && uid() in resource.data.participants;

      // ensureChat() may create the document for a conversation the caller is
      // starting; the caller must be in the participant list it writes.
      allow create: if signedIn() && uid() in request.resource.data.participants;

      // sendMessage() bumps the chat header (last message, unread count) as
      // part of sending, so any participant may update.
      allow update: if signedIn() && uid() in resource.data.participants;

      // CORRECTED 2026-09-30 — delete is denied.
      //
      // This previously allowed any participant to delete the chat document.
      // Nothing in the app does that: `firestore_chat_service.dart` contains
      // exactly one `.delete()` and it belongs to `deleteContact()`. Clearing a
      // chat is local-only (the Hive mirror). Allowing the remote delete would
      // let one participant destroy the shared conversation record for
      // everyone, leaving the messages orphaned under a dead chat id.
      allow delete: if false;

      // --- messages ---------------------------------------------------------
      //
      // The participant check has to read the parent chat. This is a
      // subcollection rule, so the get() is allowed for both get and list
      // (watchMessages() orders by timestamp within one chat).
      match /messages/{messageId} {

        function isParticipant() {
          return signedIn()
            && uid() in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/chats/$(chatId)).data.participants;
        }

        allow read: if isParticipant();

        // Only the sender may write a message, and only under their own id.
        allow create: if isParticipant() && request.resource.data.senderId == uid();

        // CORRECTED 2026-09-30 — UPDATE IS NOT SENDER-ONLY.
        //
        // Two writers rewrite a message the recipient did not send:
        //     markChatRead()        -> { status: 'read', readAt }
        //     updateMessageStatus() -> { status, deliveredAt | readAt }
        // both driven by the RECIPIENT (`chats/{chatId}/messages` is read with
        // `.where('senderId', isNotEqualTo: readerUid)` to find what to mark).
        //
        // The previous rule, `resource.data.senderId == uid()`, denied every one
        // of those writes. It would have failed QUIETLY: `markChatRead()`
        // catches the error and falls back to clearing only the caller's own
        // counter, so the read badge would simply stop clearing on the other
        // device and nothing would reach the logs.
        allow update: if isParticipant()
          && (
            resource.data.senderId == uid()
            || request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                 .hasOnly(['status', 'deliveredAt', 'readAt'])
          );

        // "Delete for everyone" stays sender-only; the one-hour window is
        // enforced in the model (`Message.canDeleteForEveryoneAt`).
        allow delete: if isParticipant() && resource.data.senderId == uid();
      }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ calls/{id}
    //
    // Both parties write: the caller publishes the offer and its candidates,
    // the callee publishes the answer and its own candidates, and either may
    // move the status. So update is "is a party", not "is the caller".
    //
    // The ring query is `where receiverId == uid where status == ringing`, and
    // the read rule is an OR over exactly those two fields — Firestore can
    // prove the query implies the rule, so the phone still rings.
    match /calls/{callId} {

      allow read: if signedIn()
        && (resource.data.callerId == uid() || resource.data.receiverId == uid());

      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.callerId == uid();

      allow update: if isCallParty(resource.data);

      // logCall() may write a history record for a call the caller placed or
      // received; the caller may also clear their own entry.
      allow delete: if signedIn()
        && (resource.data.callerId == uid() || resource.data.receiverId == uid());
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------- status/{id}
    //
    // LOOPHOLE (read): open to any signed-in user.
    //
    // This *should* be "me or one of my contacts", and that rule is easy to
    // write — `exists(contacts/$(uid())/list/$(resource.data.userId))`. It is
    // also unusable here: watchStatuses() queries
    // `where userId whereIn [30 contact uids]`, and Firestore cannot prove a
    // per-document exists() check from that query, so the Updates tab would
    // fail with "Missing or insufficient permissions" rather than show
    // anything. Making it strict needs a fan-out feed. See GAP 4.
    match /status/{statusId} {

      allow read: if signedIn();

      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.userId == uid();
      allow update, delete: if signedIn() && resource.data.userId == uid();
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------- contacts/{uid}/list
    //
    // The address book mirror. Purely private; owner-only in both directions.
    match /contacts/{ownerUid} {
      allow read, write: if signedIn() && ownerUid == uid();

      match /list/{contactId} {
        allow read, write: if signedIn() && ownerUid == uid();
      }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ channels
    match /channels/{channelId} {

      // LOOPHOLE (read): the channel *document* is open to any signed-in user.
      //
      // It has to be. Two collection-level queries hit this path —
      // watchChannelsForMonetization() (`where monetizationStatus == pending`)
      // and the channel search — and neither filters on ownership or privacy,
      // so a data-dependent read rule is unprovable for them and Firestore
      // rejects the query outright. The channel document is treated as public
      // directory metadata (name, handle, avatar, follower count); the
      // sensitive part is the posts, which ARE protected below.
      allow read: if signedIn();

      allow create: if signedIn() && request.resource.data.ownerId == uid();

      // The owner edits their channel freely. A *follower* also updates this
      // document — setFollowing() bumps `followerCount` by ±1 — so that has to
      // be allowed too, constrained to those two fields.
      //
      // LOOPHOLE (write): the ±1 bound is checked against the value the client
      // sends, so a client can still loop the increment and inflate the count.
      // It cannot be bounded by rules alone. See GAP 5.
      allow update: if signedIn()
        && (
          isChannelOwner(channelId)
          || (
            isChannelMember(channelId)
            && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                 .hasOnly(['followerCount', 'lastUpdateAt'])
            && (
              request.resource.data.followerCount == resource.data.followerCount + 1
              || request.resource.data.followerCount == resource.data.followerCount - 1
            )
          )
        );

      // deletePost() carries the comment "Owner only — enforced by security
      // rules". Until now there were no rules; this is what makes it true.
      allow delete: if isChannelOwner(channelId);

      // --- followers: the follow edge, one document per user ---------------
      //
      // setFollowing() writes followers/{uid} and watchMembership() reads it,
      // so the document id is the caller's own uid and the rule is exact.
      match /followers/{followerUid} {
        allow read: if signedIn();
        allow write: if signedIn() && followerUid == uid();
      }

      // --- posts ------------------------------------------------------------
      match /posts/{postId} {

        // Private channels hide their posts from non-members. This is a
        // subcollection rule, so the get()/exists() in the helpers is legal
        // for the unfiltered watchPosts() list.
        allow read: if signedIn()
          && (isChannelOwner(channelId) || isChannelMember(channelId) || !channelIsPrivate(channelId));

        allow create: if isChannelOwner(channelId)
          && request.resource.data.authorId == uid();

        // Three different writers touch a post after creation:
        //   - the owner (edit)
        //   - any viewer, via recordPostView() → `viewCount`
        //   - any reactor, via reactToPost() → `reactions.<kind>`
        // A non-owner update is therefore constrained to those two fields.
        allow update: if isChannelOwner(channelId)
          || (
            isChannelMember(channelId)
            && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                 .hasOnly(['viewCount', 'reactions'])
          );

        allow delete: if isChannelOwner(channelId);

        // --- reactions: one per user, document id is their uid -------------
        //
        // CORRECTED 2026-09-30 — read is own-only, and that is exactly what the
        // only query needs. `watchMyReactions()` is a COLLECTION-GROUP query:
        //     .collectionGroup('reactions').where('uid', isEqualTo: uid)
        // Its filter implies this rule, so Firestore can prove it for every
        // candidate document. Widening it to `signedIn()` also "works" but lets
        // anyone enumerate who reacted to what across every channel; the tallies
        // the UI shows come from the denormalised `reactions` map on the post,
        // so nothing needs to read other people's rows.
        match /reactions/{reactorUid} {
          allow read: if signedIn() && resource.data.uid == uid();
          allow create, update: if signedIn()
            && reactorUid == uid()
            && request.resource.data.uid == uid();
          // reactToPost() deletes the row when the same reaction is toggled off.
          allow delete: if signedIn() && reactorUid == uid();
        }
      }

      // --- join requests for a private channel -----------------------------
      //
      // requestJoin() writes join_requests/{uid}; the owner lists them all and
      // deletes on approve/reject. Both sides are expressible exactly.
      match /join_requests/{requesterUid} {
        allow read: if signedIn() && (requesterUid == uid() || isChannelOwner(channelId));
        allow create: if signedIn() && requesterUid == uid();
        allow update: if signedIn() && requesterUid == uid();
        allow delete: if signedIn() && (requesterUid == uid() || isChannelOwner(channelId));
      }

      // --- analytics: one bucket per calendar day --------------------------
      //
      // Read is owner-only (watchAnalytics). Write is deliberately open to any
      // member: bumpAnalytics() is called from the follower's own device on
      // follow, post, reaction and view, so the counter is client-reported and
      // cannot be made trustworthy by rules. See GAP 5.
      match /analytics/{dayKey} {
        allow read: if isChannelOwner(channelId);
        allow write: if isChannelMember(channelId);
      }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------ channel index documents
    //
    // channel_usernames/{handle} and channel_keys/{sha256(secret)} are written
    // in the SAME batch as the channel document they point at (createChannel),
    // so at rule-evaluation time that channel does not exist yet and get()
    // returns nothing — ownership simply cannot be verified on create.
    //
    // LOOPHOLE: create is therefore open to any signed-in user, bounded only
    // by the payload shape. A malicious client can pre-claim a handle by
    // pointing it at a channel id that does not exist, which createChannel()
    // then refuses to overwrite — a denial-of-handle. See GAP 6.
    //
    // Update and delete CAN be verified, because by then the channel exists.

    match /channel_usernames/{handle} {
      allow read: if signedIn();

      allow create: if signedIn()
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['channelId', 'name', 'createdAt'])
        && request.resource.data.channelId is string;

      allow update, delete: if signedIn()
        && resource.data.channelId is string
        && channelDoc(resource.data.channelId).ownerId == uid();
    }

    // The digest of a private channel's secret key. Anyone who can compute the
    // digest already knows the secret, so open read leaks nothing extra; it is
    // the join path (findChannelIdBySecretKey).
    match /channel_keys/{digest} {
      allow read: if signedIn();

      allow create: if signedIn()
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['channelId', 'createdAt'])
        && request.resource.data.channelId is string;

      allow update, delete: if signedIn()
        && resource.data.channelId is string
        && channelDoc(resource.data.channelId).ownerId == uid();
    }

    // Anything not matched above is denied. Firestore denies by default in
    // rules_version 2; there is deliberately no catch-all `allow` here.
  }
}

// ============================================================================
//  KNOWN GAPS — each one needs an app or schema change, not a rule change
// ============================================================================
//
//  GAP 1 — users/{uid} exposes fcmToken, publicKey and points to every
//    signed-in user, because Firestore rules cannot restrict reads by field.
//    Fix: split the public directory into its own collection (e.g.
//    `profiles/{uid}` holding only name/handle/avatar/bio/verified) and keep
//    `users/{uid}` owner-only. Every directory read in RemoteContactService
//    then points at `profiles`.
//
//  GAP 2 — the points balance is written by the client on both sides of a
//    gift. Any user can set any balance. Fix: move claimAdReward(), sendGift()
//    and the balance mutation into a Cloud Function using the Admin SDK, then
//    drop the `points` exception from the users update rule.
//
//  GAP 3 — usernames/emails/phones are an enumeration oracle: any signed-in
//    user can test arbitrary addresses for an account. This is inherent to
//    client-side contact discovery. Fix: do discovery through a Cloud Function
//    that rate-limits per caller, and make the three index collections
//    owner-only (read: resource.data.uid == uid()).
//
//  GAP 4 — status/{id} is world-readable to signed-in users because
//    watchStatuses() queries contacts with whereIn and Firestore cannot prove
//    a per-document contacts check from that query. Fix: fan out on publish —
//    write a copy into `status_feed/{viewerUid}/{statusId}` for each contact,
//    then make the rule `read: if signedIn() && viewerUid == uid()`. (The
//    fan-out itself belongs in a Cloud Function.)
//
//  GAP 5 — followerCount and the analytics buckets are client-reported and
//    unbounded in aggregate. Fix: derive them server-side, or accept them as
//    vanity metrics and stop surfacing them as authoritative.
//
//  GAP 6 — channel_usernames / channel_keys cannot be ownership-checked on
//    create because createChannel() writes the channel and its indexes in one
//    batch. Fix: write the channel document first, then the two index
//    documents in a second batch; the create rule can then be
//    `channelDoc(request.resource.data.channelId).ownerId == uid()` and handle
//    squatting disappears.
//
//  GAP 7 — `calls` has no TTL. A call document whose client died mid-ring
//    stays at status `ringing` forever, so the callee's ring query keeps
//    returning it. Nothing in these rules can expire it. Fix: a scheduled
//    Cloud Function that closes calls older than ~60s.
//
//  GAP 8 — the join-requests rule is provable only because a WIDGET guards the
//    query (query #11 above). `_OwnerTools.build()` in
//    lib/features/channels/presentation/channel_screen.dart returns early with
//    `if (!isOwner) return const SizedBox.shrink();` before it watches
//    `channelJoinRequestsProvider`, so a non-owner never issues the unfiltered
//    list that Firestore would reject. That is correct today and fragile
//    tomorrow: a refactor that hoists the `ref.watch` above the guard turns a
//    working screen into a permission error.
//    Fix (app): make the guard structural — move the count into its own
//    `Consumer` widget that is only *mounted* inside the `isOwner` branch, so
//    the provider cannot be subscribed for a non-owner no matter how the build
//    method is reordered. The rule itself should stay as written.
// ============================================================================
